WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.411
A secure Axum application starts with a
route map that a reviewer can understand.

2
00:00:08.491 --> 00:00:15.238
Public routes, authenticated user routes,
administrative routes,

3
00:00:15.318 --> 00:00:19.273
health endpoints, internal callbacks,

4
00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:24.821
and service-to-service APIs should not
blur together.

5
00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:32.695
If a team cannot quickly answer which
routes are public and which routes require

6
00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:39.523
identity, the service is already harder to
review than it needs to be.

7
00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:44.256
Handlers should have clear
responsibilities.

8
00:00:44.336 --> 00:00:51.316
A handler often receives extracted input,
checks identity or context,

9
00:00:51.396 --> 00:00:55.701
calls domain logic, and returns a
response.

10
00:00:55.781 --> 00:01:02.295
That does not mean it should silently mix
parsing, authorization,

11
00:01:02.375 --> 00:01:05.749
business decisions, data access,

12
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:11.180
and response formatting in one
hard-to-review block.

13
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.169
The more sensitive the route,

14
00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:20.298
the more valuable it is to make the
sequence of checks visible.

15
00:01:20.378 --> 00:01:25.031
Shared application state is another
boundary.

16
00:01:25.111 --> 00:01:30.346
Axum makes it convenient to pass state
into handlers,

17
00:01:30.426 --> 00:01:33.567
but state should be deliberate.

18
00:01:33.647 --> 00:01:37.486
A handler should receive what it needs,

19
00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:43.499
not a grab bag of powerful clients and
configuration values.

20
00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:50.210
Database pools, API clients, key material
handles, feature flags,

21
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:58.317
and authorization helpers should be scoped
and documented so reviewers know what

22
00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:00.491
each route can reach.

23
00:02:00.571 --> 00:02:05.341
The request lifecycle should tell a clear
story.

24
00:02:05.421 --> 00:02:09.260
Where is authentication established?

25
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.714
Where is authorization enforced?

26
00:02:12.794 --> 00:02:17.214
Where does input become trusted domain
data?

27
00:02:17.294 --> 00:02:22.180
Where are tenant and ownership checks
performed?

28
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.448
Where are errors mapped to safe responses?

29
00:02:26.528 --> 00:02:33.276
A secure design makes those answers
visible through route grouping,

30
00:02:33.356 --> 00:02:40.685
middleware scope, extractor choice,
handler structure, and domain APIs.

31
00:02:40.765 --> 00:02:45.186
Treat the route flow as a trust-boundary
map.

32
00:02:45.266 --> 00:02:53.293
A request begins outside the service and
passes through network infrastructure,

33
00:02:53.373 --> 00:03:00.236
proxy behavior, Axum routing, middleware,
extraction, validation,

34
00:03:00.316 --> 00:03:07.180
authorization, domain logic, data access,
and response generation.

35
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:15.287
Each transition should either preserve a
clear invariant or add a controlled one.

36
00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:21.300
That is what makes the application
reviewable under change.
